messages:
alex_support: Hey team — customer escalated: production traffic started getting duplicate POSTs after our SDK retried following a 401 refresh path. They say billing doubled for some requests. Repro attached and happening intermittently (~1/200). Node env + TS SDK. Can someone triage?

emma_engineer: can you paste the SDK version, node version, and the exact retry config they're using? Also any request ids from logs would help.

alex_support: quick paste from their logs: 
```
2024-10-02T14:11:22Z RequestId=abc123 POST /v1/actions 401 token_expired
2024-10-02T14:11:22Z Retrying request (attempt=2) using same AbortController
2024-10-02T14:11:22Z Refresh token success
2024-10-02T14:11:22Z RequestId=abc124 POST /v1/actions 200 ok
2024-10-02T14:11:22Z Billing: charge recorded for abc123 and abc124
```
They said SDK v1.8.2 (typescript), undici v5.17.0, Node 20. Retry policy: 3 attempts, automated on 401 then re-POST. I can share a minim repro snippet they ran.

sam_devex: please paste the repro script if possible. If they're reusing an AbortController or the same Request/Body across retries that could cause weird undici behavior. Also Node 20 changed fetch internals (stream transfer).

alex_support: repro (sanitized):
```ts
// repro.ts
import fetch from 'node-fetch'; // consumer uses undici via SDK
async function send(body: Buffer) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.com/v1/actions', { method: 'POST', body, signal: controller.signal });
  if (res.status === 401) {
    // token refresh then retry using same controller
    // BAD: reusing signal and body
    await refreshToken();
    return fetch('https://api.redwood.com/v1/actions', { method: 'POST', body, signal: controller.signal });
  }
  return res;
}
```
They confirmed they re-used the body Buffer variable but reused the same AbortController across retries.

raj_sre: hypothesis: undici + Node20 native fetch allow signal transfer but if the earlier request aborted mid-stream the body may already be partially consumed or the stream state mutated; SDK retry creating a new outgoing TCP request while a prior one is still in-flight could produce duplicate POSTs. There's also a known undici bug with signal handoff and non-rewindable bodies in some versions.

emma_engineer: agree. Immediate mitigations:
- ask customer to add an `Idempotency-Key` header for POSTs until we fix SDK behavior (best short-term).
- ensure they create a new AbortController per attempt.
- for non-rewindable bodies, buffer into a Buffer/Uint8Array and recreate the body for each retry: `const bodyBuf = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(payload)); fetch(url, { body: Buffer.from(bodyBuf) })`

sam_devex: repro script we recommend to customer to validate locally (uses node's undici fetch):
```js
// repro-undici.js
import { fetch } from 'undici';
const payload = JSON.stringify({ x: 1 });
async function send() {
  const buf = Buffer.from(payload);
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const r1 = await fetch('https://httpbin.org/status/401', { method: 'POST', body: buf, signal: controller.signal });
  // simulate refresh
  // then retry using same controller to reproduce
  return fetch('https://httpbin.org/post', { method: 'POST', body: buf, signal: controller.signal });
}
send().then(r => console.log('done', r.status)).catch(e => console.error(e));
```
This tends to show duplicated server-side entries when the first request is aborted/partial.

alex_support: customer tried the idempotency workaround and saw no duplicate charges during stress test. They want a fix in the SDK though. Blocking customer on Dedicated contract renewal for this behavior.

emma_engineer: plan: quick patch to SDK: 1) always instantiate a fresh `AbortController` per retry attempt; 2) ensure the SDK clones/serializes body for each attempt (buffer non-rewindable streams) when retrying POSTs; 3) add guard so we don't re-issue POST if previous attempt got a 2xx/409/other terminal code before retry logic kicks in. I'll draft a PR and run tests.

raj_sre: also add conditional telemetry: if retry triggered after 401 and we then see two distinct request ids within ~1s, emit an internal alert to help spot duplicates. Could be temporary.

emma_engineer: ETA: small change, unit + integ tests, and release candidate by EOD tomorrow if nothing blocks. Will backport to 1.8.x patch branch too.

deploy-bot: ⚠️ Heads up: assigning issue #INF-2387 to emma_engineer and raj_sre.

alex_support: thanks — I'll relay workaround and timeline to the customer. Please @mention me in the PR so I can follow. :thumbsup:

emma_engineer: ack — will include a repro test that demonstrates the duplicate POST when reusing the controller so customer can validate post-patch.

raj_sre: quick note for ops: when contacting customer logs, search for consecutive RequestId patterns (e.g., abc123 -> abc124) and correlate billing entries. That helped us here.

alex_support: closing loop here for now, will reopen if customer sees the issue after patch.
